WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.568
-You know, it's mid-September

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.737
and that means --

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:06.706
[ Chuckle ]
You okay, Tariq?

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:08.441
You look bothered.

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:09.576
-I'm good.

00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:11.076
[ Laughter ]

00:00:11.076 --> 00:00:12.144
-You sure?

00:00:12.144 --> 00:00:13.779
You don't seem good.

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:16.382
-No, I, uh, I just got
some things on my mind.

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:19.452
Stuff that I'm probably
gonna put on my Irk List.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.821
[ Light laughter ]

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:24.557
-What's an Irk List?

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:25.925
-You know.

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:29.495
It's the list that I keep
of things that annoy me.

00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:30.930
Random stuff.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.498
You know what?
I'll just show you.

00:00:32.498 --> 00:00:34.900
It's time
for "Tariq's Irk List"!

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.869
♪ Tariq's Irk List ♪

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:38.137
♪ They're the things that irk ♪

00:00:38.137 --> 00:00:41.374
♪ Tariq ♪

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:44.210
-Yeah, man, I'm beginning
to show some telltale signs

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.381
of aging, I guess, because I
find myself needing to use more

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:51.517
of the elderly accoutrements,
you know?

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:53.019
Reading glasses...
[ Laughter ]

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:54.954
-...back scratcher,

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:56.322
shoehorn,

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:57.990
banisters.
-Banisters.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:01.427
-And that irks me, man,
so I put it on my Irk List.

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:02.695
-Oh, I gotcha.
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:05.265
-I guess walking around with it,
first and foremost,

00:01:05.265 --> 00:01:07.833
is kind of an indication
that something ain't right.

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:11.471
Because I find myself wanting
to issue these irk tickets

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:13.139
I guess, like citations.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:14.841
-You're gonna
give out irk tickets

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:16.409
-Yeah, yeah, you know.
-to things that irk you?

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.312
-I guess I'm like
the nonwhite privileged

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:21.380
Black male version of a Karen.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.816
[ Laughter ]
I'm like a Blaren, Jimmy.

00:01:23.816 --> 00:01:25.218
-Blaren.
-Yeah.

00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:26.719
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:29.155
Like...
-Blaren.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:32.625
-...it irks me how texting is
changing the way we communicate.

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:35.462
I feel like it undermines the
usage of proper English grammar

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:37.897
and I hate that I'm stuck
in this like,

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:40.333
this text abbreviation matrix.

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.902
-I agree.
-A friend of mine texted me
the other day...

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:49.976 line:0%
And I hate that I knew
that that meant

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:52.545 line:0%
"I don't mess with people
who mess with people
I don't mess with."

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:53.646 line:0%
-Wow!

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:55.915 line:0%
[ Laughter ]
[ Miming explosions ]

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.885
-Like I wanted to issue
myself an irk ticket

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:00.753
just for understanding it.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:04.490 line:0%
So, I send "smh,"
which translates to this guy...

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.891 line:0%
-Uh-huh.

00:02:05.891 --> 00:02:07.627 line:0%
-You know what I'm saying?
And then, you know, my friend,

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:10.963 line:0%
he hits me back, "lol
Hey, man, I get it.

00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:11.964
nbd

00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:13.366
There's a new normal

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:14.567
and we're all apart of it."

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.602
And then my blood
started boiling because

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:20.739
it really irks me when someone
means to say "a part"

00:02:20.739 --> 00:02:23.742
but then they write
"apart," instead.

00:02:23.742 --> 00:02:26.512
-Wait, wait, I'm sorry.
So, a part of it, like what?

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:28.047
-Yeah, like, "apart,"
like, you know what I'm saying?

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:32.284
If you intend to text me
"a part," two words,

00:02:32.284 --> 00:02:34.420
but, you know, I mean,
you write "apart,"

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.288
like, you know what I'm saying?
Like, separate.

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:37.557
Like apart.
-Yes.

00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:39.058
Doesn't mean together.
It means apart.

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:40.693
-It irks me!
-Yeah.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:41.694
[ Laughter ]
-It irks me.

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:43.029
So, I was this close

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:45.397
to texting him back, "ilmf,"

00:02:45.397 --> 00:02:47.099
to say,
"Irk List, mother[bleep]."

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:48.100
[ Laughter ]
-Alright, alright, hold on.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.335
-But I didn't. I didn't.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:50.569
-I wanna know what is happening.

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:52.938
[ Laughter ]
You know.

00:02:52.938 --> 00:02:54.340
-What we can do here.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.675
Well, did you at least have
a nice weekend or did it...?

00:02:56.675 --> 00:02:58.344
-Well, Michelle,
she was really exited for us

00:02:58.344 --> 00:02:59.645
to go to her friend's house

00:02:59.645 --> 00:03:02.281
to do this socially distant
birthday brunch thing.

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:03.983
And I'm glad it's
socially distant

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:08.087
because her friend's husband
has breath that irks me.

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:10.156
[ Laughter ]
-Okay. His breath irks you?

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:11.156
-Yeah, yeah.
I mean, pretty much.

00:03:11.156 --> 00:03:12.191
I mean, you'd think that,

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:14.092
with such a violently oppressive

00:03:14.092 --> 00:03:17.696
wave of toxicity emitting
from one's face at all times,

00:03:17.696 --> 00:03:19.399
that they'd have some idea

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:21.601
of the carnage being left
in their wake, but,

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.502
[ Laughter ]
nope, not this guy.

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:25.004
He's clueless.

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:29.309
So I go, between the distance
and his mask, and then my mask,

00:03:29.309 --> 00:03:31.543
and then this guy,
this shield right here,

00:03:31.543 --> 00:03:33.579
I mean, there's at least like
a few different layers

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:37.283
of defense, so, I should be
safe, I guess, but then...

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:38.517
-Yeah, good idea.

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:40.553
-...what if he faints?
-What?

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:43.056
-Because doesn't he make
himself woozy?

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:45.391
[ Laughter ]

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:46.558
Because he's breathing
all that in.

00:03:46.558 --> 00:03:47.760
I mean, at this point,

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.495
people are probably thinking
he has vertigo.

00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:51.430
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.600
So I say, you know,
"It's dangerous.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.869
I mean, how many people
are coming, 20?

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:58.771
Do they even have
that kind of space outside?"

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.840
And then, she's all,
"No, it's inside,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.109
but there's only two couples
allowed per room.

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:04.777
Come on, it'll be painless.

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:06.879
We haven't gone anywhere
since March."

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.414
So I said, "Alright, cool."

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:10.716
And then, she says,
"Oh, and by the way,

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:12.418
they're a shoes-off household."

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:13.686
-Oh, no, no, no.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:16.222
-I mean, I'm fine if like
one thing leads to another

00:04:16.222 --> 00:04:17.623
and people's shoes
just come off.

00:04:17.623 --> 00:04:21.060
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.795
-[ Mouths "What?" ]

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:24.696
-But I hate if it's mandatory.

00:04:24.696 --> 00:04:26.332
Which is a little crazy because

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:28.100
I don't allow shoes
in my own home.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.470
But, see,
I offer the alternative

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.906
of some nice, chic, decorative
booties that cover the shoe.

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:35.874
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, God.

00:04:35.874 --> 00:04:38.310
-You offer that?
-Yeah, I offer that at home.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.978
You come over,
I give you options.

00:04:39.978 --> 00:04:41.514
This couple does not.

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:44.817
So, naturally I'm thinkin',
"What socks am I gonna wear?"

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.318
-Yeah.
-Because, I mean, in my mind,

00:04:46.318 --> 00:04:48.688
it's gonna be this big
sock reveal upon arrival.

00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:50.923
[ Laughter ]
But I digress.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:52.258
-Please digress.

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:54.961
-So I go, "I gotta get
some new socks."

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:57.630
I mean, the one
solid option I have

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:01.200
is my go-to Paul Smith
midrise black,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.335
but with the playful,
colorful stripe

00:05:03.335 --> 00:05:04.703
-Yeah.
-pair, and that covers

00:05:04.703 --> 00:05:06.972
all the bases,
but it has a huge hole in it.

00:05:06.972 --> 00:05:08.441
And you can't do go
to someone's house,

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:10.976
especially for a sock event,
wearing holey socks.

00:05:10.976 --> 00:05:14.080
[ Laughter ]
-Sock event.
-It's offensive. It's offensive!

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.948
Now, it's too late to do like

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:17.849
an Amazon Prime thing
or anything.

00:05:17.849 --> 00:05:19.185
And, then, with COVey,

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:21.521
I'm not going into like
a mall or anything.

00:05:21.521 --> 00:05:22.854
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.854 --> 00:05:25.391
So I gotta do a curby
someplace to grab some socks!

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:26.692
-Alright, wait, a curby?

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:29.028
-Yeah, Jimmy, a curbside pickup!

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:32.131
-Curby.
-So I ask, "What's being served?

00:05:32.131 --> 00:05:33.833
I mean, is it like a casual,

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:35.601
waffles and eggs
sort of get-together

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:38.771
or more of a, you know,
roast chicken and nice salmon,

00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:40.206
you know what I mean?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:40.206 --> 00:05:42.508
-You know, like sort of event
and she says, "It's the latter,"

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.577
which immediately
irks me even more

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:47.146
because I remember the last
time we saw this family.

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:49.248
They brought food
to our house for a barbecue

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:52.051
and it was a mostly melon
fruit salad,

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:54.587
which is an irk ticket,
no question!

00:05:54.587 --> 00:05:55.888
[ Laughter ]
-[ Claps ]

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:59.559
-I mean, who likes honeydew
or cantaloupe, right?

00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:01.460
It's more like can't-uh-nope

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.562
[ Laughter ]
and honey-don't! Right?!

00:06:03.562 --> 00:06:06.365
But to show up
with the Melony Griffith

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:08.401
is just -- it's inconsiderate!

00:06:08.401 --> 00:06:10.002
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:13.072
-Melanie Griffith?
-Yeah, melony-ass fruit salad,

00:06:13.072 --> 00:06:15.073
Jimmy.
Come on, stay with me, here.

00:06:15.073 --> 00:06:16.943
So, look, at that point,
you'd be better off

00:06:16.943 --> 00:06:18.844
just bringing paper plates.

00:06:18.844 --> 00:06:20.913
So, so, anyway, I mean,

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:24.216
I find the perfect pair of socks
available for the curby.

00:06:24.216 --> 00:06:25.651
So, I'm grabbing them
on the way

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:27.353
and I'm literally
gonna keep it moving.

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:29.488
I stop at the store.
Some kid tosses the socks

00:06:29.488 --> 00:06:31.290
into the back seat
and, when I pull over

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.059
to pull them apart,

00:06:33.059 --> 00:06:35.194
they're sewn together.
-I know that, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:37.897
-So I ask Michelle,
"Hey, look into my bag

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:38.931
and get a pair of scissors."

00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:40.432
She's going through my bag.

00:06:40.432 --> 00:06:42.635
There's the shoehorn,
there's the back scratcher,

00:06:42.635 --> 00:06:44.904
my reading glasses,
hand sanitizer,

00:06:44.904 --> 00:06:46.571
extra masks, rubber gloves,

00:06:46.571 --> 00:06:48.073
hazmat suit, lip balm,

00:06:48.073 --> 00:06:49.809
hot sauce, glue gun,

00:06:49.809 --> 00:06:51.243
[ Laughter ]
ear and nose hair trimmer.

00:06:51.243 --> 00:06:52.979
Everything but the scissors!
-Mm!

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:54.414
-So, I just did a Hail Mary

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:56.782
and I tried to just
tear 'em apart.

00:06:56.782 --> 00:06:58.551
I'm saying, you know, I tried
to catch 'em off-guard

00:06:58.551 --> 00:07:00.319
with just like a sudden tug
on the thread,

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:01.753
like pulling a loose tooth,

00:07:01.753 --> 00:07:03.456
and it was a fail, Jimmy.

00:07:03.456 --> 00:07:06.091
They tore!
So I wound up having to wear

00:07:06.091 --> 00:07:07.693
my brand-new socks
-Okay.

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:10.496
-with the huge, brand-new hole
in 'em, anyway.

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:13.065
So brunch, it was basically
an irksville this weekend.

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:15.801
But, I'm trying to stay
glass half full, man.

00:07:15.801 --> 00:07:17.736
Other than that, I'm awesome.

00:07:17.736 --> 00:07:18.804
-[ Slaps desk ]

00:07:18.804 --> 00:07:21.407
[ Laughter and applause ]
-Yeah!

00:07:21.407 --> 00:07:23.075
-"But I digress."

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:25.278
Tariq Trotter, everybody!

00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:26.379
"Irk List"!
[ Slaps desk ]

